1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,330
This city should not exist.

2
00:00:02,480 --> 00:00:04,810
It stands in the middle
of a salt-water lagoon.

3
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:06,660
There's no solid ground under it.

4
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:08,730
Just water, sand, and soft mud.

5
00:00:09,552 --> 00:00:13,482
And yet, for more than a thousand years,
it has held up heavy stone palaces,

6
00:00:13,612 --> 00:00:14,962
churches and bell towers.

7
00:00:15,212 --> 00:00:16,642
All of them, standing on mud.

8
00:00:17,394 --> 00:00:19,304
The secret is right under your feet.

9
00:00:19,614 --> 00:00:21,264
Under these streets, there's a forest.

10
00:00:21,694 --> 00:00:24,194
Millions of tree trunks, pushed
straight down into the mud,

11
00:00:24,234 --> 00:00:26,604
and they're still down
there, holding up the city.

12
00:00:27,471 --> 00:00:29,841
Wood that has been under
water for hundreds of years.

13
00:00:29,891 --> 00:00:31,341
So why hasn't it rotted away?

14
00:00:31,451 --> 00:00:32,521
Why doesn't Venice sink?

15
00:00:32,591 --> 00:00:34,361
And here's the twist: it is sinking.

16
00:00:34,671 --> 00:00:35,961
Just not the way you think.

17
00:00:39,991 --> 00:00:43,161
To understand Venice, start
with the question nobody asks:

18
00:00:43,311 --> 00:00:44,821
who builds a city in a swamp?

19
00:00:44,991 --> 00:00:46,261
People who are running away.

20
00:00:47,047 --> 00:00:50,577
In the fifth and sixth centuries,
as the Roman Empire fell apart,

21
00:00:50,967 --> 00:00:52,437
armies swept into northern Italy.

22
00:00:52,727 --> 00:00:56,617
People from the mainland towns fled to
the only place the armies couldn't follow:

23
00:00:56,767 --> 00:00:57,477
the lagoon.

24
00:00:57,747 --> 00:01:01,297
Low muddy islands, hidden channels,
water too shallow for warships.

25
00:01:02,221 --> 00:01:04,781
Only locals knew where
the safe channels were.

26
00:01:04,821 --> 00:01:06,841
To an invader, the lagoon was a maze.

27
00:01:06,881 --> 00:01:09,471
That made it the perfect
place to hide, and later,

28
00:01:09,561 --> 00:01:10,931
the perfect place to trade.

29
00:01:11,836 --> 00:01:12,406
It worked.

30
00:01:12,516 --> 00:01:15,556
For about eleven hundred years,
Venice was an independent republic,

31
00:01:15,596 --> 00:01:18,406
ruled by an elected
leader called the Doge.

32
00:01:18,676 --> 00:01:22,806
Its ships carried silk, spices, salt
and glass between Europe and the East,

33
00:01:22,916 --> 00:01:26,086
and this city on mud became one
of the richest places on Earth.

34
00:01:26,485 --> 00:01:30,255
Its shipyard, the Arsenal, was the
biggest industrial site in Europe.

35
00:01:30,585 --> 00:01:34,315
At its peak, it is said to have
employed around sixteen thousand people,

36
00:01:34,425 --> 00:01:37,195
and in wartime it could turn
out close to one ship a day,

37
00:01:37,365 --> 00:01:39,555
centuries before the
factory assembly line.

38
00:01:40,431 --> 00:01:44,081
Today Venice is spread across
more than a hundred small islands,

39
00:01:44,131 --> 00:01:47,641
split by canals and joined by
around four hundred bridges.

40
00:01:48,051 --> 00:01:51,361
But every one of those islands had
to be made strong enough to build on.

41
00:01:53,723 --> 00:01:54,703
Here's the problem.

42
00:01:54,743 --> 00:01:57,073
The top layer of the lagoon is soft mud.

43
00:01:57,203 --> 00:01:59,523
Put a stone house on it,
and it slowly sinks in,

44
00:01:59,563 --> 00:02:01,073
like a boot in wet sand.

45
00:02:01,897 --> 00:02:04,007
But a few meters down,
there's something better:

46
00:02:04,097 --> 00:02:08,047
a layer of hard, compacted clay
that the Venetians called caranto.

47
00:02:08,337 --> 00:02:09,607
The trick was to reach it.

48
00:02:10,351 --> 00:02:11,481
There were no machines.

49
00:02:11,831 --> 00:02:15,631
Teams of workers lifted a heavy weight
on ropes and dropped it onto the top of a

50
00:02:15,671 --> 00:02:19,341
pile, again and again, until the
pile was driven deep into the ground.

51
00:02:19,731 --> 00:02:20,621
Then the next one.

52
00:02:20,731 --> 00:02:21,321
And the next.

53
00:02:22,255 --> 00:02:25,105
So the builders took tree
trunks, mostly larch,

54
00:02:25,275 --> 00:02:28,835
oak and alder, sharpened them, and
hammered them straight down into the mud,

55
00:02:28,875 --> 00:02:31,725
one next to the other, as
close as they could fit them.

56
00:02:32,553 --> 00:02:36,433
Packed that tightly, the trunks squeeze
the mud between them until the whole block

57
00:02:36,473 --> 00:02:37,423
becomes hard.

58
00:02:37,773 --> 00:02:41,343
The ground under the building turns
into something closer to solid rock.

59
00:02:42,137 --> 00:02:44,807
On top of the trunks went
a flat wooden platform.

60
00:02:45,017 --> 00:02:47,567
On top of that, blocks
of white Istrian stone,

61
00:02:47,797 --> 00:02:49,987
a stone that almost doesn't let water in.

62
00:02:50,077 --> 00:02:52,307
And only above the
waterline, the brick walls.

63
00:02:53,342 --> 00:02:55,282
Look at almost any building on the canals,

64
00:02:55,322 --> 00:02:58,102
and you can see it: a white
stone band at the bottom,

65
00:02:58,142 --> 00:03:00,352
where the water touches,
and brick above it.

66
00:03:00,522 --> 00:03:02,692
That white line is the
building's raincoat.

67
00:03:03,543 --> 00:03:04,953
And the numbers are crazy.

68
00:03:05,223 --> 00:03:07,633
The Rialto Bridge, finished
in fifteen ninety-one,

69
00:03:07,683 --> 00:03:10,033
stands on about twelve
thousand wooden piles.

70
00:03:10,887 --> 00:03:14,297
The church of Santa Maria della
Salute, built after a plague,

71
00:03:14,387 --> 00:03:16,737
is said to stand on more
than a million of them.

72
00:03:17,573 --> 00:03:19,423
All that wood had to come from somewhere.

73
00:03:19,793 --> 00:03:23,673
Whole forests in the mountains to the north
were cut and floated down rivers to the

74
00:03:23,713 --> 00:03:24,303
lagoon.

75
00:03:24,693 --> 00:03:28,043
Venice is, quite literally,
a forest turned upside down.

76
00:03:30,367 --> 00:03:31,937
Now, the obvious question.

77
00:03:32,167 --> 00:03:33,087
Wood in water rots.

78
00:03:33,127 --> 00:03:36,707
Leave a wooden post in a lake, and
in a few years it's soft and black.

79
00:03:36,747 --> 00:03:38,377
So why is Venice still standing?

80
00:03:39,297 --> 00:03:41,867
You can actually see the
danger out in the lagoon.

81
00:03:42,197 --> 00:03:45,867
Those wooden posts sticking out of the
water mark the safe channels for boats.

82
00:03:46,257 --> 00:03:50,337
Out there, in open water, they're worn
down by the tides and chewed by tiny

83
00:03:50,377 --> 00:03:53,107
shipworms, and they have to
be replaced again and again.

84
00:03:53,446 --> 00:03:54,076
Quick test.

85
00:03:54,186 --> 00:03:56,546
Myth or true: the piles
under Venice never rot,

86
00:03:56,586 --> 00:03:58,496
because there's no oxygen in the mud?

87
00:03:59,090 --> 00:03:59,960
Partly true.

88
00:04:00,150 --> 00:04:02,800
Most of the fungi that
destroy wood need oxygen.

89
00:04:03,070 --> 00:04:05,030
Deep in the wet clay, there's almost none,

90
00:04:05,070 --> 00:04:06,540
so the fungi can't survive.

91
00:04:06,850 --> 00:04:09,080
That's why the piles have
lasted for centuries.

92
00:04:10,080 --> 00:04:11,850
But they're not frozen in time.

93
00:04:12,620 --> 00:04:16,440
Scientists from the University of Padua
found that bacteria can still eat the wood,

94
00:04:16,480 --> 00:04:18,790
very slowly, even without oxygen.

95
00:04:19,180 --> 00:04:22,210
The outside of a pile softens,
while the core stays strong.

96
00:04:23,119 --> 00:04:24,609
So why doesn't the city collapse?

97
00:04:24,779 --> 00:04:27,329
Deep in the mud, the shipworms
can't reach the piles.

98
00:04:27,419 --> 00:04:29,049
And the water saves them twice.

99
00:04:29,539 --> 00:04:31,489
The mud around the piles holds them tight,

100
00:04:31,559 --> 00:04:34,549
and the water that fills the softened
wood helps it keep its shape.

101
00:04:34,779 --> 00:04:36,109
The forest is still working.

102
00:04:36,199 --> 00:04:37,409
Just slowly getting older.

103
00:04:39,732 --> 00:04:42,472
And every brick, every stone,
every one of those piles,

104
00:04:42,512 --> 00:04:43,902
came here by boat.

105
00:04:44,052 --> 00:04:45,782
Because in the historic center of Venice,

106
00:04:45,852 --> 00:04:46,842
there are no cars.

107
00:04:47,052 --> 00:04:47,682
No buses.

108
00:04:47,912 --> 00:04:49,602
You're not even allowed to ride a bicycle.

109
00:04:50,662 --> 00:04:52,922
The buses here are
boats, called vaporetti,

110
00:04:52,962 --> 00:04:55,732
stopping at floating platforms
along the Grand Canal.

111
00:04:56,162 --> 00:04:57,092
Taxis are boats.

112
00:04:57,282 --> 00:04:58,252
The police are boats.

113
00:04:59,102 --> 00:05:02,192
If you call an ambulance, a
boat comes, siren and all,

114
00:05:02,262 --> 00:05:04,452
and races through the
canals to the hospital.

115
00:05:04,802 --> 00:05:06,572
The fire brigade has boats too.

116
00:05:06,782 --> 00:05:10,692
Even the rubbish is collected by boat
every morning and taken to another island.

117
00:05:11,704 --> 00:05:15,654
Every shop, every restaurant and
every hotel is supplied by water.

118
00:05:16,284 --> 00:05:19,074
Cargo boats bring in food,
drinks, building materials,

119
00:05:19,184 --> 00:05:20,494
even washing machines.

120
00:05:20,664 --> 00:05:24,034
Then it's all moved by hand trolley,
up and down the steps of the bridges.

121
00:05:25,111 --> 00:05:27,141
Even the last journey is by water.

122
00:05:27,451 --> 00:05:30,371
The city's cemetery is an
entire island, San Michele,

123
00:05:30,411 --> 00:05:32,541
and funerals arrive there by boat.

124
00:05:33,270 --> 00:05:34,160
And the gondola?

125
00:05:34,230 --> 00:05:37,480
In the sixteenth century, Venice
had around ten thousand of them.

126
00:05:37,690 --> 00:05:39,450
Today, there are only about four hundred,

127
00:05:39,490 --> 00:05:41,740
and almost all of them carry tourists.

128
00:05:44,166 --> 00:05:45,536
So the foundations hold.

129
00:05:45,706 --> 00:05:48,036
Then why do people keep
saying Venice is sinking?

130
00:05:48,286 --> 00:05:49,006
Because it is.

131
00:05:49,046 --> 00:05:50,436
Just not because of the wood.

132
00:05:51,153 --> 00:05:54,233
In the nineteen fifties and
sixties, factories on the mainland,

133
00:05:54,273 --> 00:05:58,943
at Porto Marghera, pumped huge amounts
of fresh water out of the ground below.

134
00:05:59,313 --> 00:06:02,623
The ground under the lagoon compressed,
like a sponge being squeezed.

135
00:06:03,606 --> 00:06:06,636
In about twenty years, Venice
dropped around twelve centimeters.

136
00:06:07,026 --> 00:06:08,416
That doesn't sound like much.

137
00:06:08,626 --> 00:06:10,916
For a city that sits
barely above the water,

138
00:06:11,026 --> 00:06:11,696
it's enormous.

139
00:06:12,699 --> 00:06:15,299
In the early nineteen seventies,
the pumping was stopped,

140
00:06:15,339 --> 00:06:17,529
and the fast sinking stopped with it.

141
00:06:17,619 --> 00:06:19,939
But the ground under Venice
still settles a little,

142
00:06:19,979 --> 00:06:21,309
naturally, every year.

143
00:06:22,112 --> 00:06:24,402
And at the same time, the sea is rising.

144
00:06:24,692 --> 00:06:27,222
Put the two together, and over
the last century and a half,

145
00:06:27,292 --> 00:06:31,072
the water around Venice has risen by about
two and a half millimeters every year

146
00:06:31,112 --> 00:06:32,222
compared to the city.

147
00:06:32,772 --> 00:06:34,282
Slow, but it never stops.

148
00:06:36,737 --> 00:06:39,527
And that's how Venice gets
its most famous problem:

149
00:06:39,817 --> 00:06:40,487
acqua alta.

150
00:06:40,617 --> 00:06:41,167
High water.

151
00:06:41,766 --> 00:06:45,606
In autumn and winter, a high tide,
a strong wind from the south,

152
00:06:45,646 --> 00:06:47,676
and low air pressure can all line up.

153
00:06:47,946 --> 00:06:51,896
The Adriatic is pushed into the lagoon,
and the water simply walks into the city.

154
00:06:52,802 --> 00:06:55,112
Venice even has its own alarm for it.

155
00:06:55,262 --> 00:06:58,442
When a dangerous tide is coming,
sirens sound across the city,

156
00:06:58,482 --> 00:06:59,712
a few hours ahead.

157
00:06:59,982 --> 00:07:03,092
One tone means the water will reach
a hundred and ten centimeters.

158
00:07:03,462 --> 00:07:04,932
Two tones, a hundred and twenty.

159
00:07:05,202 --> 00:07:07,572
Four tones means a
hundred and forty or more.

160
00:07:08,388 --> 00:07:10,958
The lowest place in the
city is St Mark's Square.

161
00:07:11,268 --> 00:07:14,598
The water starts coming up through the
drains at around eighty centimeters,

162
00:07:14,808 --> 00:07:16,118
so the square floods first.

163
00:07:16,468 --> 00:07:19,368
That's why you see tourists
walking on raised wooden walkways,

164
00:07:19,408 --> 00:07:21,018
or wading in rubber boots.

165
00:07:21,439 --> 00:07:22,809
And it's getting more frequent.

166
00:07:23,079 --> 00:07:26,799
In the nineteen fifties, the water went
above a hundred and ten centimeters less

167
00:07:26,839 --> 00:07:28,489
than twice a year on average.

168
00:07:28,839 --> 00:07:31,669
In the last decade, it's
more than ten times a year.

169
00:07:31,839 --> 00:07:34,549
In twenty nineteen alone, it
happened twenty-eight times.

170
00:07:34,972 --> 00:07:37,682
Every time, salt water
soaks into the walls.

171
00:07:37,952 --> 00:07:42,442
When it dries, the salt crystallizes inside
the bricks and slowly breaks them apart.

172
00:07:42,692 --> 00:07:45,702
That's why so many Venetian
walls look like they're peeling.

173
00:07:46,577 --> 00:07:49,517
The worst flood on record came
in November nineteen sixty-six:

174
00:07:49,557 --> 00:07:52,067
a hundred and ninety-four centimeters.

175
00:07:52,968 --> 00:07:54,908
Then, on the night of
the twelfth of November,

176
00:07:54,948 --> 00:07:57,378
twenty nineteen, it almost happened again.

177
00:07:58,188 --> 00:07:59,538
A hundred and eighty-seven centimeters.

178
00:07:59,908 --> 00:08:02,078
Water poured into St Mark's Basilica.

179
00:08:02,448 --> 00:08:04,158
Boats were thrown onto the pavements.

180
00:08:04,428 --> 00:08:06,318
The city declared a state of emergency.

181
00:08:08,740 --> 00:08:11,750
For decades, Venice had a
plan for exactly that night.

182
00:08:11,880 --> 00:08:12,830
It was called MOSE.

183
00:08:13,040 --> 00:08:15,310
And in twenty nineteen,
it still wasn't working.

184
00:08:16,116 --> 00:08:17,266
Here's how it works.

185
00:08:17,516 --> 00:08:20,386
The lagoon is connected to the
sea through three openings.

186
00:08:20,756 --> 00:08:24,616
On the seabed, in those openings,
lie seventy-eight giant steel gates,

187
00:08:24,656 --> 00:08:26,146
hollow, and full of water.

188
00:08:27,063 --> 00:08:29,653
When a big tide is
coming, air is pumped in.

189
00:08:29,863 --> 00:08:31,983
The water is pushed
out, the gates float up,

190
00:08:32,023 --> 00:08:35,833
and in about half an hour, a wall of
steel separates the lagoon from the sea.

191
00:08:36,739 --> 00:08:40,739
On October the third, twenty twenty, the
gates were raised for the first time to

192
00:08:40,779 --> 00:08:42,109
stop a real tide.

193
00:08:42,399 --> 00:08:45,279
And for the first time in
centuries, the sea outside rose,

194
00:08:45,319 --> 00:08:46,869
and the city stayed dry.

195
00:08:47,750 --> 00:08:51,480
By March twenty twenty-five, MOSE
had been raised a hundred times.

196
00:08:51,790 --> 00:08:53,920
But it came very late, and very expensive:

197
00:08:54,210 --> 00:08:58,230
more than six billion euros, and every
single raising costs hundreds of thousands

198
00:08:58,270 --> 00:08:58,800
more.

199
00:08:59,696 --> 00:09:01,826
The gates only rise for big tides.

200
00:09:02,276 --> 00:09:05,106
Smaller floods still reach the
lowest corners of the city.

201
00:09:05,436 --> 00:09:08,626
So in twenty twenty-two, St Mark's
Basilica got its own protection:

202
00:09:08,776 --> 00:09:12,666
a wall of glass all around it, to
keep out the water that MOSE lets in.

203
00:09:13,514 --> 00:09:14,404
And there's a catch.

204
00:09:14,574 --> 00:09:17,804
Every time the gates close, the
lagoon is cut off from the sea.

205
00:09:18,274 --> 00:09:20,824
Ships can't get in, and the
water can't refresh itself.

206
00:09:20,994 --> 00:09:24,284
The more the sea rises, the more
often the gates will have to close.

207
00:09:26,775 --> 00:09:29,045
But the water isn't the
only thing flooding Venice.

208
00:09:29,493 --> 00:09:33,413
Every year, somewhere between twenty-five
and thirty million visitors come to

209
00:09:33,453 --> 00:09:33,883
Venice.

210
00:09:34,313 --> 00:09:37,963
On the busiest days, the narrow
streets jam like a stadium exit.

211
00:09:38,803 --> 00:09:41,773
Meanwhile, the people who
actually live here are leaving.

212
00:09:42,163 --> 00:09:46,243
In nineteen fifty-one, the historic center
had about a hundred and seventy-five

213
00:09:46,283 --> 00:09:47,273
thousand residents.

214
00:09:47,683 --> 00:09:51,233
In twenty twenty-two, it fell below
fifty thousand for the first time.

215
00:09:52,029 --> 00:09:54,999
Rents went up, apartments
turned into holiday rentals,

216
00:09:55,129 --> 00:09:57,899
and shops for residents
became shops for tourists.

217
00:09:58,449 --> 00:09:59,699
Locals moved to the mainland.

218
00:10:00,599 --> 00:10:04,779
So in twenty twenty-four, Venice became
the first city in the world to charge day

219
00:10:04,819 --> 00:10:08,529
visitors just to walk in: five
euros on the busiest days,

220
00:10:08,839 --> 00:10:10,349
ten if you book at the last minute.

221
00:10:12,717 --> 00:10:13,867
So, why doesn't Venice sink?

222
00:10:14,289 --> 00:10:16,359
Because under the
palaces, there's a forest.

223
00:10:16,789 --> 00:10:19,339
Millions of trunks, driven
into the clay by hand,

224
00:10:19,409 --> 00:10:21,779
packed so tight they turned mud into rock.

225
00:10:21,949 --> 00:10:23,629
And they're still down there, in the dark,

226
00:10:23,669 --> 00:10:25,419
doing their job after centuries.

227
00:10:26,418 --> 00:10:28,888
The real threat to Venice
isn't under the city.

228
00:10:29,078 --> 00:10:31,378
It's all around it: a
sea that keeps rising,

229
00:10:31,418 --> 00:10:33,228
and a city that keeps emptying.

230
00:10:33,919 --> 00:10:36,589
Venice was built by people
running from danger.

231
00:10:36,799 --> 00:10:40,209
For more than a thousand years, it
has found a clever way to survive.

232
00:10:40,539 --> 00:10:43,049
The question now is whether
it can find one more.

233
00:10:43,945 --> 00:10:47,105
So, would you still visit Venice, or
would you leave it to the Venetians?

234
00:10:47,145 --> 00:10:49,415
And which city should we explain next?

235
00:10:49,545 --> 00:10:50,535
Tell me in the comments.

236
00:10:51,025 --> 00:10:52,135
This was How It's Run.
